      "mergeID": "5d5f1a61297e8704245bdcb6",
      "mergedPostDetails": "Sometimes, you make a mistake during T-pose calibration, and your FBT calibration is a bit off. Or the avatar may enter T-pose in an inconvenient location, such as partially clipping into an object, making it hard to align with the avatar. It would be very convenient if there was a faster way to re-enter T-pose to fix up calibration than switching into another avatar and back.",
      "mergedPostTitle": "Add button to re-trigger FBT T-pose calibration",
      "parentID": "5c358385e92910158f1f3104",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "597ce375fb356b48bff51dcf",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5a6ffbd6337de65dbe2d92a1|full_name}: \n\nI appreciate the notes but because you directed them at me I will respond with this,\n\n- yes I use playspace mover, now why should I use a third party workaround to something that can easily be fixed be spawning the avatar correctly at the hmd position. I dont have this issue, but its good to know the workaround I guess. Also this is a bad idea as it could mess up the calibration height if the playspace is not moved properly\n\n- whats so hard about this? its most likely attaching the trackers as an IK follow point, not that hard to store the information as they are already doing it anyway. You can switch to a non-humanoid avatar then back to your previous humanoid and all the calibration will still be there. In addition as long as you dont calibrate on a new avatar you can switch back and the calibration will still be there. They just need to store it externally per avatar id in a file or whatever solution they choose.\n\n- I have already done the temporary neck fix. As far as im aware vrchat is aware of the full body IK issues and should be getting a fix out because the neck weight painting does not fix the view point not staying in the head. Its already been mentioned that the head is not 100% weighted to the viewpoint IK which is one of the causes for this issue and something we cant fix with just the neck workaround. The neck fix also makes the bends look horrendous",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
